    "proof": "Put pi=sqrt(2), rho=2*pi-1 and delta=3+pi=epsilon*rho. For a primitive r=(x,y), reflection integrality is q(r)|2I(r), I(r)=(rho*x,(y,S26)). Since S26 is unimodular, every prime away from (pi) and (rho) is excluded by primitivity. At (pi), rho is a unit and I(r) is the unit ideal, so v_pi(q) is 0, 1, or 2. At p=(rho), the exponent is zero unless y lies in p*S26; in that case primitive x is a p-unit and q=-rho*x^2 mod p^2, so the exponent is exactly one. Class number one and U^+=U^2 give the six canonical totally-positive representatives (1,2+pi,2) times (1 or 3+pi)."
  },
